  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nAt 20Z/2 pm, a deepening 1006 mb low pressure was near Clinton \nIL in DeWitt county with cold front passing east of I-55 early \nthis afternoon. Temperatures had warmed into the upper 60s/lower\n70s in eastern IL ahead of the cold front. Models continue to \nshow some increase in moisture from the sw with steepening lapse\nrates with cold air aloft arriving with approaching upper level\ntrof. SB CAPEs increase to 600-1100 J/kg in eastern IL during \nthis afternoon while effective wind shear values are 25-35 kts \nin eastern IL and get to 35-50 kts in southern/se IL into \nwestern KY, just south of CWA. Higher storm relative helicities \nof 100-150 m2/s2 are over the Wabash river valley and eastward \ninto IN where marginal risk of tornadoes are. SPC continues a \nmarginal risk of severe storms 5-15% large hail and damaging \nwinds in eastern IL mainly east of I-57, and upgraded the Wabash\nriver valley to a slight risk over much of Lawrence and eastern\nparts of Clark and Crawford counties through 6 pm where 15% risk\nof large hail and damaging winds. SPC has a 2% to less than 5% \nrisk of tornadoes near the Wabash river valley. See SPC recent \nmesoscale discussion Number 2449 for eastern IL for further \ndetails.\n\nLow pressure to continue to deepen as it tracks quickly ESE \ninto west central IN by mid evening and into sw Ohio by\nmidnight. As the low deepens and tracks into the Ohio River \nValley, winds will become breezy north to NW and bring in much\ncolder temperatures next few days. Lows overnight in the upper\n30s and lower 40s coolest nw CWA. A 2nd strong northern stream \nshort wave trough over central Ontario Province will drop\nsouthward across the central Great Lakes and Ohio Valley on Thu\nand will keep more low clouds around along with isolated to\nscattered showers especially in eastern IL on Thu. Band of lake\nenhanced showers off Lake Michigan late Thu and Thu night to\nmostly be ne of CWA, but may affect far ne Vermilion county by\nHoopeston where even snowflakes possible. Brisk NNW winds \ngusting 20-30 mph Thu (NBM has 20-30% chance of gust over 30 mph\nespecially from Lincoln northeast). High temperatures Thu in \nthe mid to upper 40s central IL and lower 50s in southeast IL \nand only in the low to mid 40s Friday (likely the coldest day of\nthe season so far) despite partly to mostly sunny skies Friday.\nThe brisk winds will make it feel colder both Thursday and \nFriday. Lows Thu night in the upper 20s/lower 30s, coldest in \nwestern CWA. Widespread hard freeze expected over CWA during \nFriday and Sat nights with lows in the mid 20s (even lower 20s \nwestern CWA Friday night). Still chilly Saturday with highs in \nthe mid to upper 40s. \n\nThe seasonably cool weather likely lingers early next week with\nhighs in the upper 40s/lower 50s Sunday and Monday as dry\nweather prevails. Large mid/upper level trof over the Eastern \nUS late this week gradually shift away from IL during early\nnext week with weak upper level ridging into the MS river \nvalley and Eastern Plains. Surface high pressure of 1030-1034 mb\nto settle near IL by Monday and shift into the mid Atlantic \nStates by middle of next week. Temperatures to slowly moderate \nback to near or slightly above normal in the middle to upper 50s\nby next Wednesday, and near 60F next Thursday.",
